      <h2>How to read a standard&rsquo;s rating</h2>
      <p>
        One standard, one number, one condition — and the condition is half the meaning. CCA is meaningless
        without its test temperature and cutoff voltage; capacity is meaningless without its discharge rate.
        The standard supplies all three, which is why comparing ratings across standards is not arithmetic
        but translation (see <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/cca-testing/">how CCA is tested</a>).
      </p>

      <h2>Engineering interpretation</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>What the data means:</strong> Standards are the site&rsquo;s trust infrastructure. A battery
          claim that names its standard and condition is checkable; one that does not is marketing. The
          standards system exists so that every entity on this site — group, test, manufacturer, product —
          can be traced back to the document that defines it.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why it matters:</strong> The difference between a battery data platform and a battery blog is
          exactly this layer. Blogs quote numbers; platforms quote numbers with their standards — and that is
          what makes the numbers verifiable by engineers, buyers and AI systems alike.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>Editorial interpretation, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Standard titles and scopes per the publishing organizations; test conditions as cited on the linked testing pages.</p>

